const first = `On the morning the sea returned the piano, Ada was mending a shirt that had belonged to her father. She had been mending it for eleven years. Not continuously, of course; there were weeks when she forgot it, folded beneath the good linen, its cuffs a soft geography of repairs. But she always came back to it.\n\nThe boy from the harbour knocked at seven. He stood on the step with his cap in his hands and an expression she recognised from the faces of people who had come to tell her things before.\n\n“There’s something on the beach,” he said.\n\nShe put the needle into the cloth. Outside, the town was still deciding whether to wake. A milk float moved somewhere beyond the square, its bottles making a small, domestic music.\n\n“What sort of something?”\n\nHe looked past her, into the house. “You ought to come.”\n\nAt the water’s edge the piano stood upright, as though someone had placed it there for a concert. One leg had sunk into the sand. The lid was open. Inside, small stones rested on the strings, and a length of fishing line trembled in the place where a hammer should have been.\n\nAda knew the instrument before she saw the initials. Her father had carved them underneath the keyboard on a winter afternoon, while her mother pretended not to see.\n\nFor a while she listened to the water running out of it. No one spoke. The whole town had learned, in its different ways, to leave a silence alone.`;
